    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm working on a low-fat mayo prototype and the emulsion is breaking pretty quick when we drop the pH. could you explain the molecular mechanism of how lecithin stabilizes the interface, specifically in high-acid environments?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Lecithin acts as an emulsifier by having both hydrophilic and lipophilic parts, allowing it to reside at the oil-water interface. In high-acid environments, the negatively charged phosphate and choline head groups on lecithin can become protonated, which alters its surface charge and interaction with the dispersed droplets. This change can reduce electrostatic repulsion, causing the unstable droplets to coalesce and lead to emulsion breaking.",
